            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies the 'lost sheep' as any spiritually straying person or sinner whom God compassionately seeks and restores. It also broadly recognizes a specific, secondary application referring to the covenant people of Israel.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book strictly limits the symbol's referent to the scattered house of Israel, emphasizing corporate exile and regathering.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A straying sinner or person who has wandered from God.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The scattered house of Israel.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies the symbol universally to all repentant sinners or straying humanity, while the book restricts the referent exclusively to the national/corporate house of Israel.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction in the underlying metaphor (straying and being sought); the disagreement lies entirely in the scope of who is represented by the sheep.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a recognized alternative meaning (Israel) to the sole definition, preserving the thematic core of straying and seeking but radically narrowing the extension by rejecting the universalized application to any individual sinner.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The parables in Luke 15 and Matthew 18 are traditionally and contextually read as applying to individual sinners and vulnerable disciples generally, not solely as allegories for the ethnic or historical house of Israel.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
